WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:02.360
Hello everyone, welcome back to another episode

00:02.360 --> 00:06.200
of the Grand Prixquel Podcast where we discuss all things feeder series.

00:06.200 --> 00:10.640
On today's episode, we are going to be doing a recap of the Italian F4 and FREC weekends

00:10.640 --> 00:11.640
at Monza.

00:11.640 --> 00:21.120
Your hosts for today are myself, Robin, Nova, Peter, and Alex.

00:21.120 --> 00:23.240
So do we want to start with Italian F4?

00:23.240 --> 00:26.000
I mean, a lot happened.

00:26.000 --> 00:31.080
I mean, going into the weekend, we were hoping that we could maybe return to the normal 3-race

00:31.080 --> 00:38.440
format, because Monza's capacity, well, it was 49 cars, it had 49 entries.

00:38.440 --> 00:41.440
4-race format pisses me off really bad.

00:41.440 --> 00:44.440
Yeah, but I've since learned that Monza has

00:44.440 --> 00:48.160
actually reduced their capacity to 36 cars from 49.

00:48.160 --> 00:50.360
I think it's just for this.

00:50.360 --> 00:53.760
Yeah, I mean, after that Imola start last year

00:53.760 --> 00:55.360
it was to prevent that ‘cause.

00:55.360 --> 00:59.800
I mean, even with less cars on track, they were all a bit crazy.

00:59.800 --> 01:03.040
But also, I mean, it's not feeder series,

01:03.040 --> 01:08.660
but like just a few weeks back, we saw GT World challenge and it was like a huge crash

01:08.660 --> 01:15.640
into the first turn, specifically because we all know the first turn is the end of every

01:15.640 --> 01:17.680
single racing driver ever.

01:17.680 --> 01:25.340
So I think it really makes sense to, you know, try to give every single driver three races

01:25.340 --> 01:34.080
if they qualify, instead of saying, you know, what's more likely that you get taken out,

01:34.080 --> 01:39.080
especially if you have such a huge grid and crashes would be even more.

01:39.080 --> 01:42.000
Yeah, like, the races were already chaotic

01:42.000 --> 01:47.280
enough as is with like 30-something drivers, imagine all 49 cars, that would be horrific.

01:47.280 --> 01:51.000
Yeah, so in qualifying, I mean, the US racing

01:51.000 --> 01:58.840
dominance kind of continued with Edu Robinson on pole overall, and also with Noah Killion

01:58.840 --> 02:00.800
on pole for race one.

02:00.800 --> 02:08.600
Qualifying was nice in that Andy qualified P3 in his group, however, when it came to

02:08.600 --> 02:15.200
the first race, he was meant to start P3, got a two place penalty for what was it, the

02:15.200 --> 02:21.160
going over the maximum time allowed in qualifying, and like half the grid got penalties for it,

02:21.160 --> 02:22.340
two places each.

02:22.340 --> 02:29.920
So I remember Andy got one, Emily got one, I think Maccagnani got one, there were a lot

02:29.920 --> 02:30.920
of drivers.

02:30.920 --> 02:33.880
Penalties are gonna be a common theme this weekend.

02:34.280 --> 02:38.360
Yeah, so Andy started P5 instead of P3, which

02:38.360 --> 02:40.160
I'm a bit upset about.

02:40.160 --> 02:44.360
I mean, Bondarev has finally had a decent qualifying.

02:44.360 --> 02:45.360
Finally.

02:45.360 --> 02:48.600
Yeah, he's been struggling a lot it seems,

02:48.600 --> 02:53.400
I mean last weekend, the previous weekend, he qualified like P27 or something.

02:53.400 --> 02:55.680
He's just too attached to his number.

02:55.680 --> 02:59.160
Yeah, but race one, I mean, race one was,

02:59.160 --> 03:04.040
I think the race I enjoyed the most this weekend from Italian F4, because there was

03:04.040 --> 03:08.120
so much happening, and we actually had racing up the front.

03:08.120 --> 03:11.960
Can people stop taking Consani out of races,

03:11.960 --> 03:12.960
please?

03:12.960 --> 03:14.560
I'm tired, I'm done!

03:14.560 --> 03:16.480
That's just the French luck.

03:16.480 --> 03:17.960
I'm done!

03:17.960 --> 03:21.080
Like, genuinely, every single French driver

03:21.080 --> 03:25.280
has so much bad luck, it's, how do you even do that?

03:25.520 --> 03:30.600
It doesn't help that he drives for R-ace GP, there's already the bad luck there.

03:30.600 --> 03:33.360
What did the French do to annoy the motor racing

03:33.360 --> 03:34.360
gods?

03:34.360 --> 03:37.480
Like, what did they do to deserve all of this?

03:37.480 --> 03:42.040
Because, I mean, I think it's impossible to name a French driver with good luck.

03:42.040 --> 03:44.240
I'm always making fun of Germany, because

03:44.240 --> 03:48.560
I think it's a really, really interesting topic of how racing's here, but the thing

03:48.560 --> 03:54.800
is, for some reason, the French are even worse at it, and the French have even more bad luck,

03:55.280 --> 04:01.280
and in Germany you can point most of it down to the fact that we don't have any support

04:01.280 --> 04:06.640
anymore and stuff, but the French have fucking French F4, which is like organized by this

04:06.640 --> 04:11.240
whole organization, but it's still just, how do you do that?

04:11.240 --> 04:17.480
How do you have so many also French drivers, generally, but not a single one of those has

04:17.480 --> 04:18.840
any type of luck?

04:18.840 --> 04:22.120
It's, the season has been miserable as an

04:22.120 --> 04:29.720
Andy fan, it's horrible, I'm done, cancel Italian F4 2026, we don't need that anymore.

04:29.720 --> 04:38.120
But yeah, so when Andy got taken out by Killion, somehow Bondarev also got involved, and they

04:38.120 --> 04:39.120
both DNF'd.

04:39.120 --> 04:45.480
I don't remember how Bondarev was involved, but I think he went off behind Andy to avoid

04:45.480 --> 04:47.280
crashing into him or something like that.

04:47.280 --> 04:50.040
Yeah, it basically happened right in front

04:50.040 --> 04:53.680
of him, and there's nothing he could really do.

04:53.680 --> 04:58.560
Unfortunate for both him and Consani, they were kind of, I mean, as normal we'd just

04:58.560 --> 05:02.360
blame it on US racing, because we don't like them, do we?

05:02.360 --> 05:06.680
Andy and Bondarev are both kind of interlinked,

05:06.680 --> 05:11.160
they both suffer together, I don't know what is going on over there, but they're both suffering

05:11.160 --> 05:12.160
together.

05:12.160 --> 05:14.200
I was gonna say that, I was gonna say every

05:14.200 --> 05:20.160
race, one of them DNF's, the other one DNF's either immediately or as a reaction or cause

05:20.160 --> 05:21.160
of that.

05:21.160 --> 05:23.160
No, it's not because of it, it's because

05:23.160 --> 05:28.760
they're always DNF, because I feel like I'm looking at the stats side, and actually

05:28.760 --> 05:33.760
Bondarev has not DNF'd so much this season, but I feel like every single time we talk

05:33.760 --> 05:37.760
about him, it's like, oh, he had a bad time, he was taken out, he crashed.

05:37.760 --> 05:42.120
Because it's at the end of the races, so technically

05:42.200 --> 05:46.800
they're both classified, but they didn't finish the race.

05:46.800 --> 05:49.360
And I feel like also we think it could be a

05:49.360 --> 05:54.640
bit more on him, because I think the expectations for him were quite high this year, and

05:54.640 --> 05:57.640
Yeah, everyone just said he would definitely

05:57.640 --> 06:04.080
win, but at the moment, I wouldn't even be sure if he finishes in the top 10, because

06:04.080 --> 06:09.600
he is on like P9 right now on the championship, or wait, that's the old-

06:10.480 --> 06:13.560
P13 actually, yeah, he scored one point

06:13.560 --> 06:15.560
this weekend, so- Oof

06:15.560 --> 06:19.800
One point, I can't even laugh, Andy scored

06:19.800 --> 06:20.800
nothing!

06:20.800 --> 06:23.640
There's so much happening, I remember also in

06:23.640 --> 06:29.000
race 1, okay, Payton also got killed by God knows who, it's probably one of the US

06:29.000 --> 06:30.000
racing cars.

06:30.000 --> 06:32.520
I don't know who car number 6 is, I need to

06:32.520 --> 06:33.520
check that.

06:33.520 --> 06:35.520
Wait, let me check, I can see.

06:35.520 --> 06:36.520
6 is Oscar Repetto.

06:37.160 --> 06:40.160
Yeah, so they got involved in an incident

06:40.160 --> 06:41.160
together.

06:41.160 --> 06:43.880
And also, I mean, the Thomas Bearman subplot

06:43.880 --> 06:48.800
this race is crazy, he was just doing a bit of, like, I need to watch this race and

06:48.800 --> 06:54.760
Only pay attention to him, because he was going off-roading a bit, you know, the thing at

06:54.760 --> 07:00.360
the end where he broke his front wing somehow, he was going up and down the order, and somehow

07:00.360 --> 07:03.760
he finished in the points, I don't even know anymore.

07:03.760 --> 07:11.360
Savinkov was on the podium, and I don't like that, I'm not a fan of that.

07:11.360 --> 07:14.360
And it was an US racing 1, 2, 3.

07:14.360 --> 07:16.360
Cancel the championship.

07:16.360 --> 07:17.360
Nuke it.

07:17.360 --> 07:18.360
Nuke IF4.

07:18.360 --> 07:21.160
It should have been Kenzo, but he got a penalty

07:21.160 --> 07:22.160
for something.

07:22.160 --> 07:25.520
His penalty, what was it for?

07:25.520 --> 07:26.520
I think it was-

07:26.520 --> 07:29.360
Couldn't figure out the Italian F4 Docs website.

07:29.360 --> 07:33.280
I think Kenzo's penalty was for, um, leaving

07:33.280 --> 07:35.600
the track and gaining an advantage, I think?

07:35.600 --> 07:39.120
I mean, I don't care, let him do that.

07:39.120 --> 07:41.880
Exactly, free him, he's already had enough

07:41.880 --> 07:42.880
of a bad time.

07:42.880 --> 07:44.880
He did nothing wrong.

07:44.880 --> 07:45.880
Exactly.

07:45.880 --> 07:46.880
Free my son.

07:46.880 --> 07:49.480
But I think we should point out what we talked

07:49.480 --> 07:52.000
about before.

07:52.000 --> 08:00.080
Because last year, like, Prema and US Racing were pretty up there, but I feel like, especially

08:00.080 --> 08:08.120
this weekend, US Racing was just so far ahead and like so incredibly strong, because obviously

08:08.120 --> 08:11.080
part of it is also that they have like, I think they have like 7 cars.

08:11.080 --> 08:13.080
They do have 7 cars.

08:13.080 --> 08:15.080
Prema has 7 as well?

08:15.080 --> 08:17.600
Prema has 7 as well, right?

08:17.600 --> 08:20.880
Yeah, we also, we, I'm not part of the team,

08:20.880 --> 08:22.880
Prema also has 7 cars.

08:22.880 --> 08:26.080
The problem is that all of the US Racing drivers

08:26.080 --> 08:30.280
have a bunch of experience, they're all 2nd or 3rd year drivers.

08:30.280 --> 08:35.120
Meanwhile, Prema's lineup consists of 5 rookies, Bondarev and Cosma.

08:35.120 --> 08:38.800
I feel like we should not call Ary Bansal and

08:38.800 --> 08:45.080
Arjen Kraling like 2nd year drivers, because I know they did like technically F4 last

08:45.840 --> 08:47.840
season, but GB4 hardly counts.

08:47.840 --> 08:51.840
I mean, it's the same with Killion as well.

08:51.840 --> 08:53.840
I mean Killion came from AU4.

08:53.840 --> 08:55.840
Yeah, Killion came from Australia.

08:55.840 --> 08:59.240
Okay, we should also not count him.

08:59.240 --> 09:03.160
But I also think so, it's at least some experience

09:03.160 --> 09:06.480
which is more than the Prema drivers have.

09:06.480 --> 09:08.160
Yeah, exactly.

09:08.160 --> 09:12.760
Well technically, Maccagnani isn't a rookie because he did F4 Southeast Asia last season.

09:13.000 --> 09:18.040
I wanted to say Maccagnani, wasn't he the one who tested F2 cars?

09:18.040 --> 09:20.040
Oh yeah, that's Maccagnani.

09:20.040 --> 09:22.040
I'm not counting him as a rookie.

09:22.040 --> 09:30.040
But like Aksoy did Formula Trophy, but Formula Trophy is like a fake series anyways.

09:30.040 --> 09:31.880
I watched the race in real life,

09:31.880 --> 09:33.720
you can't just say that's not real.

09:34.920 --> 09:40.600
I think I watched Aksoy win in real life, which was cool, I was right next to the podium.

09:41.560 --> 09:46.920
I think the US racing cars were just a step ahead this weekend in particular because

09:46.920 --> 09:52.440
the fact that all of their cars would be in, like, the top 15 is just kind of insane.

09:52.440 --> 09:57.800
There's reason why US racing is very, very clearly

09:57.800 --> 10:05.800
leading the team's championship and it's like that's, I mean, we still have quite a few races

10:05.880 --> 10:11.720
in front of us, but the moment it's the way it's going right now, I would not be,

10:12.360 --> 10:16.360
I would be everything but surprised if they won the team's championship.

10:16.360 --> 10:21.880
I predicted them as team's champions as a joke.

10:21.880 --> 10:23.560
They were not meant to actually win it.

10:23.560 --> 10:26.840
No, I'm not surprised.

10:26.840 --> 10:31.720
I don't think I, I'm not sure I would have to listen back if I predicted it,

10:31.720 --> 10:37.800
but I do remember that I really thought that they were for many years like the second

10:38.760 --> 10:48.360
biggest team next to Prema and if we now look at, you know, the way that both teams have a driver

10:48.360 --> 10:53.240
lineup, it really makes sense. Like yeah, even if you discount the

10:53.240 --> 10:59.000
fact that most of Prema's lineup are rookies, they're just a bit weak, unfortunately.

10:59.560 --> 11:03.480
Even the more experienced drivers aren't doing that well in Prema whereas in US,

11:04.040 --> 11:07.400
they actually are and they have that consistency which Prema lacks.

11:07.400 --> 11:10.600
Yeah, I think that's going to sound crazy,

11:10.600 --> 11:15.400
but I feel like Sammalisto is kind of doing what we all expected Bondarev to do.

11:15.400 --> 11:16.600
Oh no, he is.

11:16.600 --> 11:20.440
And Cosma is doing what I expected Consani to do.

11:20.440 --> 11:26.280
Well, I want to say one more thing about Race 1 and I'm going to have to find it,

11:26.280 --> 11:33.240
which is what I thought was really interesting to me personally. If I'm going to f***ing find it,

11:33.240 --> 11:39.800
oh my god, we have female drivers. That's really, really nice because I really like female drivers.

11:40.520 --> 11:45.960
And in Race 1, we had this weekend with Maffi Racing, I think it was,

11:45.960 --> 11:52.280
Rosanne den Drijver…I can't pronounce Dutch names. That's horrible.

11:53.000 --> 11:59.320
And she scored P17 on debut in Italian F4 which I think,

12:00.840 --> 12:04.920
if we're going to ignore the fact that Maffi racing for some reason only has one car which

12:04.920 --> 12:11.560
gets on the podium and stuff, and one car which is, I don't know if it's the driver or the team,

12:12.280 --> 12:18.760
but I still think that P17, even if aided by the fact that quite a few drivers like DNF'd and

12:19.400 --> 12:27.480
crashed and exploded and whatever, I think it was really good showing for her. And I need to point

12:27.480 --> 12:36.040
out that I saw her race in Nordic 4 some months ago and she's kind of the one that me and some

12:36.600 --> 12:44.440
other people who are really into female drivers are kind of predicting we could see her show up

12:44.440 --> 12:54.040
again in multiple races and stuff. I think she won't return in Italian F4 so fast because

12:54.040 --> 12:59.960
the car is usually driven by Ginevra Panzeri who has a lot of funding for some reason,

12:59.960 --> 13:04.680
but I think it's always worth looking out for these female drivers.

13:05.560 --> 13:11.160
Before we move on to race 2, half the grid got penalties.

13:12.920 --> 13:17.480
Three of the premas did. Aksoy, Zheng, and Maccagnani all got 10 seconds each.

13:20.040 --> 13:27.000
Each of them for causing a collision. I don't know what they fed those drivers before the race.

13:27.960 --> 13:29.720
That's the spirit of Monza.

13:29.720 --> 13:36.600
They were doing something. Killion got 25 seconds for killing Andy, which is deserved.

13:37.880 --> 13:42.920
Kenzo got 5 seconds for, like I said, I think it's for leaving the track and gaining an advantage.

13:42.920 --> 13:46.760
I could go check the doc but I can't be bothered to go check the doc. Whatever.

13:47.480 --> 13:55.720
A couple other drivers that aren't really super relevant unfortunately also got 5 seconds.

13:56.360 --> 14:00.600
I don't know if it changed the point standings at all but whatever.

14:02.920 --> 14:04.120
So race 2.

14:05.320 --> 14:07.240
The horrors continued for Consani.

14:07.800 --> 14:13.640
Italian F4 is a fake series. I don't want to talk about it.

14:15.240 --> 14:21.800
In this race, I quite enjoyed Aksoy's racecraft because he didn’t have the best qualifying. He was

14:21.800 --> 14:26.520
starting around in P12 or something around there. But I liked how he was able to make

14:26.520 --> 14:31.320
his way through the field and be able to make the right moves. I think people kind of realise

14:31.320 --> 14:36.120
Moving into turn one, you can't really make moves because you just gonna get forced off at the

14:36.120 --> 14:42.040
chicane. It doesn't work. We instead, you know, began to see drivers instead making their

14:42.040 --> 14:47.720
moves down towards Curva Grande. He made quite a few over there and I felt like he's been quite

14:47.720 --> 14:51.880
smart and he made his way up to P3 which it was quite fun to watch.

14:52.680 --> 15:03.320
I think he's pretty much–at the moment up to Vallelunga–He's won every single

15:04.200 --> 15:11.480
rookie race win. He's the rookie race winner every single time up to Vallelunga. It hasn't

15:11.480 --> 15:15.800
been updated for Monza yet which I think he will in this race probably.

15:17.640 --> 15:21.720
Only race two he would have won in the rookie in Monza because...

15:22.680 --> 15:32.600
Yeah, we're a great team so I was thinking that. But he still should have enough lead to go then.

15:32.600 --> 15:40.680
I think he should definitely win this one because this far he's won six times in a row.

15:41.480 --> 15:48.200
In every single race he participated in. So I think for the rookies he's definitely

15:48.200 --> 15:52.760
doing a lot better than… especially you predicted, Peter.

15:53.720 --> 15:58.760
He's really impressed me. He's easily been the best rookie. Not that the others have.

15:59.960 --> 16:03.080
I mean we saw Bondarev's struggles continue as well.

16:03.960 --> 16:09.400
He was fighting for the race lead and then suddenly he's off the track and then he rejoins.

16:10.040 --> 16:17.240
And before we get into the conspiracy theory, Andy was running in the brink of

16:17.880 --> 16:25.320
not qualifying for race four. He was running around P19, P18-ish. Bondarev goes off track,

16:25.320 --> 16:29.160
comes back on track in P18 and suddenly Andy is nowhere to be seen.

16:30.680 --> 16:33.560
The conspiracy theory here is that something happened between them.

16:34.360 --> 16:40.360
I don't know, maybe Bondarev came straight into Andy's path or something, I don't know.

16:41.640 --> 16:48.120
Props to Bondarev for being able to recover to P16 on track, promoted to P15 after penalties which

16:48.120 --> 16:53.480
did mean he would qualify for the final because if he had not been able to get it back on track

16:53.480 --> 16:57.320
and up that high, he would have been a DNQ for the final.

16:57.880 --> 17:02.760
It would have been really funny if Bondarev DNQ'd with Andy.

17:04.280 --> 17:06.440
But he qualified and Andy did not, so.

17:07.160 --> 17:12.520
Race two was a lot calmer than race one. Race one was like,

17:13.480 --> 17:15.800
I think maybe they realized, oh we need to be a bit more,

17:16.920 --> 17:23.480
we can't be just crashing into each other, but that's in the spirit of F4 in a way that they

17:23.480 --> 17:30.920
just love to. Walther got a drive-through penalty in the race for causing a collision. He goes

17:30.920 --> 17:35.800
and serves the penalty, but he serves it incorrectly. How do you serve a drive-through

17:35.800 --> 17:39.800
incorrectly? Maybe he sped in pitlane or something.

17:40.840 --> 17:47.240
He served the drive-through incorrectly and so got a 6 place grid drop for the next race,

17:48.280 --> 17:53.160
which I don't know whether that would have been race three or race four, but whatever.

17:53.160 --> 17:57.000
Would have been the final, I feel like, but I don't-

17:57.000 --> 17:59.160
I don't know if he participated in race one or not.

17:59.640 --> 18:04.760
Because I remember in the final he had to come back, he was starting quite far back in the final,

18:04.760 --> 18:06.600
so. Oh yeah, right, yeah.

18:07.320 --> 18:10.520
And then race three, we saw our favorite win race three.

18:13.160 --> 18:17.240
Again. He is dominating,

18:17.240 --> 18:23.000
but it's like, we've talked about this, it's just not what anybody expects. I mean this

18:23.000 --> 18:29.720
podium was, Sammalisto, Bearman and Cosma. I saw a stat on Twitter that said that if

18:29.720 --> 18:36.920
Sammalisto wins the first race in Mugello, he ties Bearman, Antonelli and Slater for

18:36.920 --> 18:42.120
most wins in a row in Italian F4. That's actually really f***ing bad.

18:42.920 --> 18:49.320
Horrible. You're tying two F1 drivers and a driver who is for sure getting to F1.

18:49.320 --> 18:52.120
I can't.

18:52.120 --> 18:58.920
But I think it's also really good for anyone who wants to discredit drivers, because, you know,

18:58.920 --> 19:06.840
I really respect all of them, but I'm just saying that if basically any guy who's in the right car

19:06.840 --> 19:10.680
can do it, maybe we should think about what that means.

19:11.320 --> 19:16.280
I think this really should show F1 Twitter that feeder series results aren't everything,

19:16.280 --> 19:19.240
and that if you have a fast enough car, you can literally do anything.

19:19.800 --> 19:23.320
Oh yeah, and that there's so much context to race results and everything.

19:24.280 --> 19:27.240
Yeah, you can't just read the wiki and say you know everything.

19:27.880 --> 19:31.400
If you're in an AKM car, you're not going to be doing that.

19:31.960 --> 19:39.160
Nobody is. You could put Lewis Hamilton in an AKM car and he's probably not winning.

19:39.480 --> 19:44.120
Put Max Verstappen in a... What the f*** is a P.A. racing car?

19:45.160 --> 19:50.680
Okay, turns out I just saw something. We need to change stuff.

19:50.680 --> 19:54.280
Ginevra Panzeri was not racing with Maffi. I swear there was

19:54.280 --> 19:56.040
somewhere else where she was racing with them.

19:57.320 --> 20:01.720
Just so we have that cleared up, because I don't want to get called out for my mistake.

20:02.360 --> 20:04.280
We've been called a lot for mistakes before.

20:05.160 --> 20:10.360
The Academy episode, I forgot to mention James Anagnostiadis

20:10.360 --> 20:14.360
in the Merc Academy section, and I feel so bad for it to this day.

20:14.920 --> 20:16.440
That's so bad, actually.

20:17.000 --> 20:18.120
I feel so bad.

20:19.320 --> 20:23.240
Actually, she actually replaced Igor Polak, who...

20:23.240 --> 20:28.120
That sounds like a f***ing... What's the word? A f***ing slur.

20:28.120 --> 20:32.040
But the good thing is, she already placed better than him, I think.

20:32.760 --> 20:35.080
No, actually they're tied, but you know.

20:35.800 --> 20:39.240
Okay, so if we are already back to talking about women,

20:40.600 --> 20:48.680
Emily Cotty and Raffaela Ferreira crashed with a guy called Kuklane.

20:48.680 --> 20:51.480
Kuklane? I don't know how you pronounce it.

20:51.480 --> 20:52.200
Kuklane.

20:52.760 --> 20:53.400
Oh, thank you.

20:54.680 --> 20:55.880
Actually, what happened there?

20:56.440 --> 20:57.480
I don't know.

20:58.360 --> 21:03.880
Suddenly, Kuklane is turning and Emily and Rafaela are both...

21:03.880 --> 21:07.080
I don't know what happened, but either way, it ended...

21:07.080 --> 21:08.040
They didn't show us.

21:09.080 --> 21:13.720
They did, but I just don't know how to describe it in a way that makes sense for the podcast.

21:13.720 --> 21:17.480
But either way, the investigation ended with no further action.

21:18.360 --> 21:21.160
So Emily deserves a gun.

21:21.160 --> 21:25.960
I think she should be freed from the hell that is Italian F4.

21:25.960 --> 21:27.480
Let her out of here, please.

21:28.440 --> 21:30.760
She should nuke one car each race.

21:33.240 --> 21:35.400
The thing is, from what I saw,

21:35.400 --> 21:42.360
I think neither her nor Ferreira would have qualified for the final anyways.

21:42.360 --> 21:48.040
Yeah, but it's better to go out with a P20 than to be taken out by another driver.

21:48.600 --> 21:52.280
Yeah, obviously, but I think it just shows that

21:52.840 --> 21:57.880
I would have expected more from R-ace as a whole because...

21:58.520 --> 22:00.760
Do not expect anything from R-ace ever.

22:01.720 --> 22:06.520
Okay, because apparently R-ace is just really, really f***ing bad.

22:06.520 --> 22:09.240
And that's so weird, actually.

22:09.960 --> 22:12.200
I don't know what goes on anymore.

22:12.200 --> 22:15.560
So we're still in race three, right?

22:15.560 --> 22:21.480
And we saw, if I saw correctly, there's no penalties or anything.

22:21.560 --> 22:24.920
Emma Felbermayr actually scored a point, unless...

22:25.960 --> 22:29.480
Oh, actually, she scores two points then, I think.

22:30.280 --> 22:34.760
Yeah, we have it on the doc, if you would check that.

22:35.400 --> 22:41.400
I'm looking at the doc, but there's like 20 different penalties also applying.

22:41.960 --> 22:45.880
And I'm not 100% sure if all penalties have been applied.

22:46.840 --> 22:51.800
If maybe penalties have been unapplied, who knows?

22:53.960 --> 22:58.920
The top 15 that we have on the doc is the corrected top 15 after penalties.

22:58.920 --> 23:00.280
Okay, okay, great.

23:00.920 --> 23:04.360
So we have points by Emma Felbermayr, which is, again, great.

23:04.360 --> 23:09.400
I think it's really sad because I expected Cotty to do better,

23:09.400 --> 23:13.000
but also, you know, f***ing R-ace GP.

23:13.240 --> 23:20.600
So she's, you know, the girl with most points, but also just like three points.

23:20.600 --> 23:22.600
That's not that much of a difference.

23:23.160 --> 23:33.640
But Westcott finally scored a point after she was close to points quite a lot of times,

23:33.640 --> 23:40.520
but I know she finished like P16, P17, P18, like around that a bit.

23:41.320 --> 23:41.720
A bit.

23:41.720 --> 23:46.680
And it was really annoying because we all know that she should be able to do better,

23:46.680 --> 23:48.760
but it's good that she finally scored a point.

23:49.320 --> 23:50.200
That's very good.

23:50.200 --> 23:50.760
I like that.

23:51.320 --> 23:57.720
And den Drijver, the name is really s***y because if you don't know it,

23:57.720 --> 24:05.640
it's spelled D-R-I-J-V-E-R, like driver, but there's a J in there.

24:05.640 --> 24:07.160
Why is there a J in there?

24:07.160 --> 24:13.240
She finished P23, which is, it's, you know, fine.

24:13.240 --> 24:15.400
It's like not, oh my God, great.

24:15.400 --> 24:20.840
But if you're in a smaller team, as like the second race she ever did in Italian F4 .

24:21.400 --> 24:23.880
I'm glad enough she didn't crash.

24:24.840 --> 24:26.760
Yeah, she had a pretty clean weekend.

24:28.600 --> 24:31.000
In terms of penalties for race three,

24:32.280 --> 24:35.560
Robinson ended up killing Savinkov at the end of the race.

24:35.560 --> 24:37.400
So he got 10 seconds for that.

24:38.760 --> 24:42.040
Bansal got five seconds for leaving the track and gaining an advantage.

24:42.040 --> 24:44.280
Hattemer also got five seconds for the same thing.

24:44.840 --> 24:49.560
So I think those two were, all three of those drivers were in the top five.

24:50.360 --> 24:52.760
So it really changed around the podium and stuff.

24:53.400 --> 24:56.360
That's why there’s less US Racing cars in the top five.

24:56.360 --> 24:58.120
They all got penalized.

24:58.120 --> 25:00.280
And that's why Bearman got a podium.

25:00.280 --> 25:05.240
Bearman finished the race in P4, but with penalties, he moved up to P2.

25:05.640 --> 25:09.880
Now Sammalisto Bearman Cosma is like the most random podium of all time.

25:10.680 --> 25:13.160
I think I'm moving on to race four now.

25:13.160 --> 25:16.040
We had quite a few drivers get grid penalties for,

25:17.400 --> 25:20.040
but I mean that they're kind of irrelevant ones.

25:20.040 --> 25:22.760
I didn’t even notice them during the race that it's kind of there.

25:23.480 --> 25:27.400
One of these drivers, Orsini, he got six places for the final.

25:27.400 --> 25:30.680
And then in the final, he got another 10 places for the next race,

25:30.680 --> 25:31.880
which will be at Mugello.

25:31.880 --> 25:33.640
I don't know what is going on over there.

25:33.640 --> 25:36.200
I have not paid a single ounce of attention to him,

25:36.200 --> 25:38.920
but he's just terrorizing everyone.

25:38.920 --> 25:41.720
In Mugello, he'll get a 12 place point penalty then.

25:41.720 --> 25:43.240
I mean, there's clearly a pattern here.

25:43.800 --> 25:46.600
I don't know what he's doing, but he's doing something.

25:47.560 --> 25:53.240
Yeah, race four and, surprise, surprise, US Racing 1-2-3.

25:53.960 --> 25:55.640
At least Costoya was P4.

25:56.200 --> 25:57.640
He finally had a good race.

25:58.200 --> 26:02.840
I mean, they were 1-2-3-5-7-9.

26:03.640 --> 26:10.760
And Kraling got killed by Aksoy, but he would have been P4-ish without that, so.

26:10.760 --> 26:13.640
That is actually so f***ing bad.

26:14.760 --> 26:15.880
This is horrible.

26:16.840 --> 26:21.560
The R-ace hell continued with Bearman killing Craigie.

26:22.280 --> 26:24.680
I actually don't know if Bearman killed him,

26:24.680 --> 26:27.320
or if it was like a racing incident or something.

26:27.320 --> 26:29.720
Or maybe Kenzo killed him.

26:30.360 --> 26:32.440
Because there was no investigation.

26:32.440 --> 26:33.960
There was nothing.

26:35.080 --> 26:36.520
Neither of them got penalties.

26:37.160 --> 26:39.240
Not even a proper replay of what happened.

26:39.960 --> 26:42.680
But we didn’t see- we just see them kind of going off.

26:42.680 --> 26:50.840
I love how you can just crash an R-ace car and the race stewards just say, you know, whatever.

26:51.800 --> 26:52.520
They hate us.

26:53.480 --> 26:55.160
It was bound to happen anyway.

26:55.160 --> 26:57.880
Given the R-ace Luck, maybe his car would have exploded.

26:58.520 --> 27:02.200
And Bearman ended up recovering to P15 while Kenzo DNF’d.

27:02.920 --> 27:06.600
I mean, Bearman had the random weekend of all times.

27:07.160 --> 27:11.080
He was just doing god knows what this weekend, but.

27:12.760 --> 27:16.440
And then, I mean, Costoya kept on cutting the chicane, which was hilarious.

27:17.000 --> 27:18.280
Genuinely, I was questioning.

27:18.280 --> 27:20.040
Does he know how to take the chicane?

27:20.600 --> 27:22.520
He literally got a black and white flag for it.

27:23.080 --> 27:28.040
Just like every single time they passed by that one chicane, Costoya cut it.

27:28.040 --> 27:30.520
He only stopped after the black and white flag, which...

27:31.240 --> 27:33.480
Maybe he didn't know the track layout.

27:33.480 --> 27:36.120
Maybe he thought that's just how you drive it.

27:36.120 --> 27:37.000
Do you know that?

27:37.000 --> 27:38.280
Have you asked him?

27:38.280 --> 27:41.000
I mean, given the chaos of turn one.

27:41.880 --> 27:43.400
I mean, it's just.

27:43.400 --> 27:46.840
He was just trying to avoid crashing into someone in turn one.

27:47.400 --> 27:51.320
Something happened to Bondarev, like we saw him pitting and his rear wing looked a bit

27:52.120 --> 27:54.520
off and he was hard from the way.

27:54.520 --> 27:56.440
We never got a replay.

27:57.320 --> 28:02.680
I mean, I assume maybe somebody went to the back of him given it was the rear wing, but who knows?

28:03.480 --> 28:04.040
It's one of...

28:04.600 --> 28:06.840
I mean, there's so many mysteries from this weekend.

28:07.720 --> 28:11.160
Race direction was kind of horrible this weekend.

28:12.760 --> 28:14.760
I mean, TV direction, sorry, not.

28:14.760 --> 28:18.840
I mean, it's been a common theme, like even in FREC, which we'll get onto,

28:19.800 --> 28:22.120
the TV direction was questionable.

28:22.680 --> 28:25.320
You know, they loved showing us replays at the wrong time.

28:26.440 --> 28:30.600
Yeah, like in the first FREC weekend when they were showing us drivers going off into

28:30.600 --> 28:33.960
the gravel, but then they wouldn't show us the fight for the lead.

28:33.960 --> 28:36.680
We still don't know what happened to KNB in race three.

28:37.320 --> 28:44.120
But I just feel like the problem with Italian F4 is also we have like 30 drivers and it's so

28:44.120 --> 28:50.120
hard to decide between Bondarev, who's somewhere not in the front and you have,

28:50.120 --> 28:52.040
you know, so much happening at once.

28:52.040 --> 28:54.840
It's always so difficult.

28:54.840 --> 28:58.840
I think, yeah, in F4, it’s more excusable but when we get onto FREC.

28:59.560 --> 29:04.280
I mean, the misdirection in FREC is a bigger issue because they're missing things at the front.

29:04.920 --> 29:07.720
We need to finish this race before we can go to FREC.

29:08.280 --> 29:12.600
And what I want to say about this race too is that I thought it was quite interesting

29:12.600 --> 29:19.960
that in the end, we had three female drivers all behind each other with Emma Felbermayr in P24.

29:20.760 --> 29:31.320
Den Drijver, something like that, P25, and Payton Westcott in P26, which I thought was

29:31.320 --> 29:37.240
especially interesting because we all kind of rate Felbermayr and Westcott and we know

29:37.880 --> 29:38.840
where to place them.

29:39.400 --> 29:47.160
But it's interesting to me that they are, you know, broken up with the driver who's name.

29:47.560 --> 29:52.760
I can’t pronounce. I think Westcott went rallycross at some point during the race.

29:53.720 --> 29:56.360
Something happened to one of the R-aces and I was like, which R-ace was that?

29:56.360 --> 29:59.080
And then it ended up being Payton, but I don't actually remember what happened.

29:59.080 --> 30:02.040
I just remember her going off the track and then rejoining.

30:03.000 --> 30:08.360
My point is that even if she had some bad luck, definitely she did, it's really interesting

30:08.360 --> 30:14.520
and it's a good sign for me that the driver whose name I can't pronounce is pretty much

30:14.520 --> 30:19.800
up there with her and also Emma Felbermayr who, you know, she scored points this weekend.

30:19.800 --> 30:31.800
I think she qualified in an alright place for the final because she finished the first two

30:31.800 --> 30:36.840
races in like P17 and P15 or P14, I guess.

30:36.840 --> 30:42.840
I don't know, but the thing is, I think she was not like dead last because I saw that

30:43.720 --> 30:50.120
they were all kind of lucky because like Aksoy, you know, fell back and some others

30:50.120 --> 30:52.600
fell behind them in the end.

30:52.600 --> 30:59.000
But the fact that just timing-wise, the driver whose name I can't pronounce managed to stay

30:59.000 --> 31:04.840
so close to a driver like Felbermayr who has scored points before who, you know,

31:04.840 --> 31:08.360
she's doing something and I think that's a very good sign for her.

31:09.240 --> 31:16.840
In terms of penalties, Aksoy gets 8 places for causing a collision with Kraling,

31:16.840 --> 31:21.400
Cosma got 5 seconds for leaving the track and gaining an advantage, and like I mentioned

31:21.400 --> 31:26.920
earlier, Orsini got 10 places for pushing drivers off track and causing collisions all in one race.

31:27.880 --> 31:30.360
That sounds so terroristic.

31:30.920 --> 31:35.080
And like he's not even a driver that anyone really pays attention to unfortunately,

31:35.080 --> 31:37.320
so no one knows what happened.

31:39.080 --> 31:42.040
But he was terrorizing the grid, apparently.

31:42.920 --> 31:47.320
And I think this is an example of, you know, the stewards seeing that a driver has done

31:48.120 --> 31:52.440
like the same kind of incident before and so making the penalty harsher than it would

31:52.440 --> 31:53.400
normally be.

31:53.400 --> 31:58.840
They did this to Andy in another weekend as well, but just if you're a repeat offender

31:58.840 --> 32:05.720
of a certain kind of incident, they will just get more harsh when they're penalizing you.

32:06.680 --> 32:11.240
But yeah, the championship is interesting, to say the least.

32:13.000 --> 32:19.240
So Wikipedia is not updated at the moment, so we've had to go off of the graphic that

32:19.240 --> 32:21.240
they show at the end of the race.

32:22.280 --> 32:25.560
So if there's anything inaccurate here, that is not our fault.

32:26.200 --> 32:29.560
The graphic and a bit of our own math to try to work it out.

32:30.200 --> 32:34.920
Yeah, because the graphic didn't include Cosma's 5 second penalty,

32:34.920 --> 32:37.320
so we've had to make a couple of adjustments for that.

32:38.360 --> 32:42.360
So currently, Sammalisto leads the championship on 228 points.

32:43.160 --> 32:47.160
Behind him is David Cosma on 181.

32:48.040 --> 32:51.800
Aksoy has fell down to P3 in the championship on 166.

32:52.920 --> 32:55.000
Was he leading the championship before this weekend?

32:55.560 --> 32:56.060
Yes.

32:56.520 --> 32:57.020
Wow.

32:57.400 --> 33:01.400
But yeah, he didn't have, I mean, he just got a lot of penalties this week, basically.

33:02.280 --> 33:05.880
Yeah, penalties and just overall not a great time.

33:07.080 --> 33:13.240
Savinkov is P4 on 150, and Bansal is P5 on 143.

33:13.240 --> 33:14.120
That's the top 5.

33:15.240 --> 33:19.000
Drivers who are not in the top 5, but I think it's still important to mention them.

33:19.000 --> 33:21.960
Maccagnani is P11 on 88 points.

33:21.960 --> 33:24.520
Bondarev P13 on 73.

33:24.520 --> 33:27.000
Craigie P14 on 68.

33:27.000 --> 33:31.160
Costoya P20 on 48, and Consani is not even in the top 20.

33:31.800 --> 33:34.280
That just sums up the season so far, I feel like.

33:35.080 --> 33:37.960
Because the graphic only shows the top 20 drivers,

33:37.960 --> 33:42.040
I could not tell you where Consani is in the championship, which is horrible.

33:42.840 --> 33:44.760
It's never been so over.

33:45.880 --> 33:47.800
There is no way we recover from this.

33:47.800 --> 33:50.920
Two DNQs in a row, we are not recovering.

33:52.440 --> 33:57.560
If we manage a top 15 in the championship by the end of the year, I will be cheering.

33:58.520 --> 34:04.600
I think right now you're a bit dramatic, because I think top 15 should still be possible.

34:05.160 --> 34:11.400
This R-ace, how do they do so well in the Middle East, and then combust in Italy?

34:11.400 --> 34:12.520
The thing is.

34:12.520 --> 34:15.640
Our car is not as bad as it was last year.

34:15.640 --> 34:20.120
It's just our drivers are A, unlucky, and B, do not know how to keep it clean.

34:20.920 --> 34:22.600
Probably a skill issue then?

34:22.600 --> 34:27.240
If you look at how R-ace qualified in Monza last year, comparing it to this year,

34:27.480 --> 34:30.920
last year our best qualifying in Monza was like a P17.

34:31.640 --> 34:33.960
And I remember Powell qualified like P27.

34:35.000 --> 34:37.960
We had Andy qualifying P3 this time.

34:37.960 --> 34:42.840
So the car is better, at least in terms of qualifying pace, it is better.

34:42.840 --> 34:44.680
It's just, we are so unlucky.

34:45.720 --> 34:46.200
I'm done.

34:46.760 --> 34:47.880
Let's move on.

34:47.880 --> 34:48.760
Let's move on to FREC.

34:49.320 --> 34:56.840
So first thing we'll talk about in FREC is that Thomas Strauven was only there for Spa and Zandvoort.

34:57.480 --> 34:59.560
So he's been replaced by Kyuho Lee.

35:00.920 --> 35:07.320
However, there is a FREC round, which clashes with both GB3 and Eurocup 3.

35:07.320 --> 35:13.080
So we will definitely be getting another new driver in that CL Motorsport seat at some point.

35:14.120 --> 35:20.760
No clue who they would put in the seat, but we're gonna get another driver that's not Strauven or Lee.

35:22.280 --> 35:26.040
And obviously not Frey either because he's like ended things with the team.

35:27.000 --> 35:31.480
And so I feel like the qualifying one, which I mean,

35:31.480 --> 35:36.120
KNB on pole and Olivieri second, kind of what we expect.

35:36.120 --> 35:41.800
But then, I mean, it is very much kind of business back to usual.

35:43.160 --> 35:46.920
The two championship leaders qualified on the third row together,

35:47.720 --> 35:48.840
Al Dhaheri and Wheldon.

35:49.640 --> 35:50.920
So they were fifth and sixth.

35:50.920 --> 35:57.320
I mean, given how Monza is, as long as you're decently in the top 10,

35:57.320 --> 35:59.560
it's fairly easy to recover positions.

36:00.440 --> 36:04.840
It's not as detrimental as maybe a circuit where you can't overtake.

36:04.840 --> 36:07.560
Then you're more kind of screwed if you aren't in that top 10.

36:08.440 --> 36:18.120
Yeah, so for race one, we had a big crash into turn one,

36:18.120 --> 36:25.880
where Giaccardi just cut the corner and just went straight into two Rodins, an R-ace.

36:26.440 --> 36:27.640
And who else was it?

36:29.160 --> 36:30.760
I don't remember who else.

36:31.400 --> 36:32.760
One of the Tridents, I think.

36:34.120 --> 36:35.400
Yeah, was it Popov?

36:36.440 --> 36:38.440
Yeah, it was Popov for race one.

36:39.240 --> 36:46.040
Yeah, so Popov, Sano, Ninovic, Seewooruthun, and obviously Giaccardi.

36:46.040 --> 36:48.440
And we're missing a driver, there was another driver.

36:49.240 --> 36:52.440
I forgot who else was involved in the crash.

36:53.320 --> 36:58.360
There was a lot happening in that first five minutes of race one.

36:59.320 --> 37:05.160
What I have here with retired drivers on the wiki page is Popov, Ninovic,

37:05.960 --> 37:14.920
and Sano, and Seewooruthun, and Macedo, and obviously Giaccardi.

37:16.040 --> 37:20.120
Macedo was involved in that.

37:21.000 --> 37:22.120
Oh yes, yes.

37:22.760 --> 37:23.960
He's the driver we were missing.

37:25.000 --> 37:26.840
So yeah, big crash on the opening lap.

37:26.840 --> 37:31.960
I'm surprised they didn't red flag it, because we had a 10 minute safety car instead.

37:33.000 --> 37:35.080
I mean, we just love safety car.

37:35.080 --> 37:36.760
I think they didn't want to red flag it,

37:36.760 --> 37:40.120
maybe because Italian F4 was scheduled right after,

37:40.120 --> 37:41.960
so that would have been delaying everything.

37:42.920 --> 37:45.880
I guess, but we got less racing because of it.

37:46.760 --> 37:48.600
Yeah, I mean, it was still quite, I mean,

37:48.600 --> 37:52.600
what was Seb Wheldon doing because he dropped to P10 on that first lap?

37:52.600 --> 37:53.960
He wanted to test himself.

37:54.680 --> 37:56.680
Probably just, I don't know,

37:56.680 --> 37:59.800
I was going to say he tried to avoid the crash,

37:59.800 --> 38:02.120
but the crash happened behind him, didn't it?

38:02.120 --> 38:04.520
Yeah, it happened behind him.

38:04.520 --> 38:05.720
I don't know what he was doing.

38:06.360 --> 38:10.280
So the commentators kept getting confused between Wheldon and Abkhazava,

38:10.280 --> 38:12.520
because they're both the two orange MPs,

38:12.520 --> 38:16.440
because the third MP is Chi, who has the Aston Martin livery.

38:16.440 --> 38:18.120
And they're running right next to each other.

38:19.000 --> 38:21.240
Yeah, because there's an incident with Al Dhaheri,

38:21.240 --> 38:24.680
which one of them, which we figured was Abkhazava,

38:24.680 --> 38:29.720
because he was the one who was next to him on track at the time,pushed him wide.

38:30.360 --> 38:34.120
Yeah, the two MPs were right behind each other,

38:34.120 --> 38:37.000
and Al Dhaheri got into an incident with the one that was behind,

38:37.720 --> 38:39.800
which the commentators kept calling Wheldon,

38:39.800 --> 38:41.160
but it was actually Abkhazava.

38:42.040 --> 38:44.920
Yeah, and this one was about the TV direction,

38:44.920 --> 38:46.040
because the TV direction,

38:46.600 --> 38:49.000
because they're basically showing KNB crossing the line,

38:49.000 --> 38:51.320
where we wanted to see the battle for P3, cause.

38:52.040 --> 38:55.560
For P3 between the three MPs was insane,

38:55.560 --> 38:57.720
and I'm so sad we didn't get to see it properly.

38:58.280 --> 39:01.320
Wheldon and Abkhazava crossed the line at the exact same time,

39:01.320 --> 39:03.320
down to the thousandth.

39:04.120 --> 39:06.200
They ended up giving it to Abkhazava,

39:06.200 --> 39:08.600
I mean, maybe, I thought they'd given it to Wheldon,

39:08.600 --> 39:10.360
because Wheldon qualified higher,

39:10.360 --> 39:13.960
but actually, no, he got outqualified by Abkhazava,

39:13.960 --> 39:17.480
that's why Abkhazava is ahead on paper.

39:18.040 --> 39:20.280
And Chi was a tenth behind them,

39:21.240 --> 39:24.040
so I'm really mad we didn't get to see that.

39:24.840 --> 39:27.880
I mean, yeah, I really enjoyed the FREC races this weekend,

39:27.880 --> 39:29.240
because even for the lead,

